window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["09"]={"https://theconversation.com/why-canada-has-a-moral-imperative-to-address-homelessness-296188":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"ebc611eb49e636d3be5969642404746c9549db4b63634ccaf4c477ddb698de78","read":"https://theconversation.com/why-canada-has-a-moral-imperative-to-address-homelessness-296188","bytes":181848,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Why Canada has a moral imperative to address homelessness","by":[{"n":"Nicole Fice","r":"Assistant Professor, School of Health Studies, Western University"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T21:18:55Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Prime Minister Mark Carney ",["has announced","https://www.pm.gc.ca/en/news/news-releases/2026/11/06/prime-minister-carney-launches-plan-put-canada-path-end-homelessness"]," a plan in the 2026 federal budget aimed at eliminating homelessness in the next decade. A statement about the plan says homelessness “strikes at the heart of Canadian values of fairness, solidarity, and human dignity.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["What are these values and how exactly are they impacted by homelessness? The announcement does not clearly answer these questions. As a researcher in public health ethics, I want to fill the gaps by drawing on empirical and philosophical research to explain why, exactly, the urgent need to address homelessness is matter of ethics."]},{"k":"h","s":["Eroding dignity"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dignity ",["refers to a kind of inherent worth","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dignity/"]," that all people hold equally by virtue of being human. It serves as ",["the foundation","https://www.un.org/en/about-us/universal-declaration-of-human-rights"]," for human rights, which provide people with entitlements and establish obligations for others (including governments) to treat people in ways that respect their rights."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violations of ",["those human rights","https://www.ohchr.org/en/special-procedures/sr-housing/homelessness-and-human-rights"],", which include a guarantee to ",["adequate housing","https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-mechanisms/instruments/international-covenant-economic-social-and-cultural-rights"],", are one way people experience a loss of dignity when homeless. Some policy responses to homeless encampments have also ",["violated rights","https://www.canlii.org/en/on/onsc/doc/2026/2026onsc2971/2026onsc2971.html?resultId=7b44d75b35444008a59045768d5ac7f7&searchId=2026-05-29T10:44:37:114/bf3fbb5c4edd40b6b4b393dce4d571f4"]," guaranteed under the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms, and therefore ",["peoples’ dignity","https://www.constitutionalstudies.ca/2022/08/human-dignity/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Homelessness violates dignity in other ways. A lack of shelter, housing and a home — ",["understood as a place of belonging","https://doi.org/10.1007/s10982-021-09414-w"]," — leads people to experience shame and ostracism."]},{"k":"p","s":["Homelessness ",["excludes people from meaningful participation","https://doi.org/10.59962/9780774865135"]," in social life. That leaves them ",["on the margins","https://crhesi.uwo.ca/margins/homelessness/"]," of society, with little to no resources or options, reduced social status and ",["a lack of freedom","https://homelesshub.ca/resource/homelessness-and-issue-freedom/"]," ",["and authority","https://doi.org/10.1080/13698230.2022.2057025"]," over their lives since much of what they do, even just to survive, is ",["criminalized","https://doi.org/10.1177/15271544231176255"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In these ways, homelessness reduces people to being less-than, unworthy, and unequal."]},{"k":"h","s":["What makes a just society?"]},{"k":"p","s":["This brings us to fairness and related questions about what a just society looks like."]},{"k":"p","s":[["In one view","https://doi.org/10.1086/233897"],", a just society involves how people stand in terms of equality with one another rather than in hierarchic relations. The failure of the state to adequately respond to homelessness is a profound injustice because ",["it demonstrates","https://philpapers.org/rec/SUNJSG"]," a lack of equal concern for some of the most vulnerable members of society. And, ",["as some argue","https://doi.org/10.1177/00323217241286789"],", this is precisely what’s at play in terms of homelessness."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another famous view comes from ",["American philosopher John Rawls","https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvjf9z6v"],". For him, justice as fairness is concerned with the distribution of benefits and burdens in society, according to ",["two principles","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/#TwoPriJusFai"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The first principle states that everyone has an equal claim to basic liberties. The relevant part of the second principle, called the difference principle, holds that any social and economic inequalities in a just society should give the greatest benefit to the least advantaged members of society."]},{"k":"p","s":["Although this view tolerates some inequality, homelessness remains incompatible with its principles of justice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Rawls didn’t name housing as one of the benefits to be distributed under these principles. ",["Some scholars","https://www.bostonreview.net/articles/housing-is-a-social-good/"]," have argued that it must be one of the social goods we should be concerned about distributing fairly, through, say, social housing models."]},{"k":"p","s":["The fact that homelessness exists to the degree it does in a wealthy country like Canada ",["does in fact suggest","https://theconversation.com/the-homelessness-scourge-in-cities-like-london-ont-exposes-the-evils-of-wealth-inequality-287016"]," a lack of fairness in a broader distributive sense. Some people are left without shelter altogether, some have subpar shelter while others have plenty."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the causes of homelessness are ",["complex and multi-faceted","https://homelesshub.ca/collection/homelessness-101/what-are-the-causes-of-homelessness/"],". The problem has been ",["made worse","https://www.statcan.gc.ca/o1/en/plus/5170-homelessness-how-does-it-happen"]," by a lack of affordable housing, higher unemployment and overall higher costs of living associated with rising inflation."]},{"k":"p","s":["In research on the causes, experts and advocates agree that it’s not caused by the choices people make, but ",["the choices we make as a society","https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph18063066"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health inequities"]},{"k":"p","s":["Such social choices have ripple effects, as people who experience homelessness face risks to their health and well-being. Homelessness disproportionately affects ",["some populations","https://homelesshub.ca/collection/population-groups/"],", which can lead to ",["vicious cycles","https://nhchc.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/homelessness-and-health-connection-fact-sheet.pdf"]," of poor health, higher risk of ",["infectious disease","https://doi.org/10.1177/00333549241228"]," and ",["premature death","https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/dyag129"],". Homelessness therefore leads to ",["inequities","https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-03727-7_3"]," in health."]},{"k":"p","s":["Homelessness is an affront to human dignity, and it’s unfair. ",["The concept of solidarity","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/solidarity/"]," speaks to the need for society generally to ",["address injustices","https://doi.org/10.5325/j.ctt7v61r"]," as a collective. Failing to do so results in ",["the social","https://doi.org/10.5749/minnesota/9780816693474.001.0001"]," and ",["moral abandonment","https://www.bloomsbury.com/ca/not-giving-up-on-people-9781538176474/"]," of people experiencing homelessness as they are left to suffer the injustices of homelessness alone."]},{"k":"p","s":["While these arguments make a compelling ethical case for ending homelessness, the real work lies in translating justification into action. In addition to ",["monitoring and evaluating","https://www.thestar.com/news/canada/carney-is-trying-to-do-what-trudeau-couldn-t-why-his-homelessness-plan-is-being-met-with-cautious-optimism/article_19aebbf2-572c-4649-b592-60962ed50e07.html"]," the practical outcomes of the promised federal funds, Canadians should not lose sight of the ethical values underpinning them and the overarching goal of ending homelessness."]},{"k":"p","s":["We need to ensure our policies not only reflect our ethical values, but also produce outcomes that actively support them. We must consider whether the policies and programs implemented align with our ethical values, and whether their outcomes are effective in promoting them as well."]}],"chars":5050},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"9f01f90d2e74b817fbd5edda3e81d08b3151d66cdf4a2dd2eab94f532f2b6759","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus","bytes":82604,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Cyprus travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-18T15:08:35+00:00","mod":"2026-08-14T10:34:05+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional tensions"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high risk of sudden escalation in the Middle East, where the regional context remains unpredictable."]},{"k":"p","s":["If affected, British Nationals should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["read ",["If you’re affected by a crisis abroad - GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],". This includes guidance on “how to prepare for a crisis” with suggestions on what you might include in your emergency supplies and “what to do in a crisis”"]},{"k":"li","s":["sign up to ",["FCDO Travel Advice email alerts","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/email-signup"]]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local and international media for the latest information"]},{"k":"li","s":["sign up to local information alerts/resources and follow the instructions of the local authorities"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep your departure plans under review, and ensure your travel documents are up to date"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about the risks of travel, to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Cyprus’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Cyprus set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Cyprus High Commission in the UK","https://cyprusinuk.com/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Cyprus, your passport must have:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it may have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago"]},{"k":"li","s":["an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"li","s":["at least one blank page for stamping"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid British passport or ",["proof of right of abode","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode"],". See ",["Dual Citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can travel without a visa to Cyprus for up to 90 days. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["At Cyprus border control, you may also need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["requirements for working in Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-cyprus-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cyprus is not part of the Schengen area, so:"]},{"k":"li","s":["visits to Cyprus do not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit in the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["visits to other EU or Schengen countries do not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit in Cyprus"]},{"k":"li","s":["Cyprus is not affected by the new Entry/Exit System (EES) that will be introduced in Schengen countries from 12 October 2025. You will not need to register for EES when entering Cyprus. Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you get your passport stamped on entry and exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If your passport is missing a stamp, show evidence of when and where you entered or left Cyprus (for example, boarding passes or tickets) and ask the border guards to add the date and location in your passport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["passport stamping if you live in Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-cyprus#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, ",["check with the Cyprus High Commission","https://cyprusinuk.com/visa-information/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not overstay your permitted time. You could be:"]},{"k":"li","s":["questioned by immigration at the airport on exit"]},{"k":"li","s":["put on a ‘stop list’ and refused entry for up to 5 years"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entry requirements for the north of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Republic of Cyprus authorities will count time in the north of Cyprus towards the 90-day visa-free total. You can use any crossing point to move between the north and south of the island."]},{"k":"p","s":["See a ",["list of the crossing points from the Cyprus Ministry of Finance","https://www.mof.gov.cy/mof/customs/customs.nsf/All/05AEEF243C9BFC8BC22572BF002D0A28?OpenDocument"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Only pedestrians can use the crossing points at Ledra Palace and Ledra and Lokmaci Street."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Republic of Cyprus through the north (such as through Ercan Airport), authorities will consider you to have entered illegally. You could be:"]},{"k":"li","s":["fined for illegal entry"]},{"k":"li","s":["refused entry to or exit from the Republic of Cyprus"]},{"k":"li","s":["prevented from crossing back into the north of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information about the north of Cyprus, see ",["Regional risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["UK residents of Cyprus or other EU countries"]},{"k":"p","s":["Residents should carry residency documents issued by the Republic of Cyprus when entering and exiting Cyprus."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have a Withdrawal Agreement residency document for another country, your passport might still be stamped if you are a visitor to Cyprus."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Cyprus guide","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/61/cyprus#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Cyprus","https://www.mof.gov.cy/mof/customs/customs.nsf/All/0D56470143C54886C22572BF002D7648?OpenDocument"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Customs rules between the north and south of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are ",["controls on quantities and types of goods","https://www.mof.gov.cy/mof/customs/customs.nsf/All/05AEEF243C9BFC8BC22572BF002D0A28?OpenDocument"]," that can be bought in the north of Cyprus and taken across the ‘Green Line’ to the south of the island. This includes goods from the bicommunal village of Pyla in the buffer zone."]},{"k":"p","s":["For example, the Republic of Cyprus enforces a 40-cigarette per person limit. The authorities can confiscate goods at the checkpoint and you could be fined."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Cyprus cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations may occur in cities with little or no notice. Events in the Middle East have led to heightened tensions and demonstrations are likely. Avoid any protests, political gatherings, or marches and leave the area if one develops. Local transport routes may be disrupted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Republic of Cyprus is a full EU member, but the country is divided by the ‘Green Line’. The British government does not recognise the self-declared ‘Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus’."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Crime against tourists is not common but take sensible precautions. Keep passports, money and other valuables safe."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Personal attacks, including sexual assaults, are infrequent but do happen. Drug-assisted rape is a risk, including with drugs like GHB and liquid ecstasy. Buy your own drinks and always keep sight of them. Avoid splitting up from your friends."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cyprus has a strictly enforced zero-tolerance policy towards illegal drugs, including laughing gas and cannabis."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should expect severe penalties such as a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Cyprus have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is legal in the Republic of Cyprus and civil partnerships are recognised. However, they may not be as widely accepted as in the UK. Same-sex sexual activity is also legal in the north of Cyprus, but not widely accepted."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dual nationals and military service"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are of Cypriot descent, even if you do not have a nationality card, authorities may consider you eligible for military service. If this happens, you could have difficulties when exiting Cyprus, and you might have to prove that you live outside Cyprus."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under international law, the British High Commission cannot intervene with Cypriot authorities on behalf of those considered to be dual Cypriot-British nationals. If you might be eligible for any local obligations or duties that apply to dual nationals, such as military service, contact the Cypriot authorities or a local lawyer before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The British High Commission has lists of English-speaking lawyers in Cyprus and in the north of Cyprus. See ",["Getting help","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/getting-help"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Surrogacy"]},{"k":"p","s":["The British High Commission cannot get involved in surrogacy arrangements."]},{"k":"p","s":["The laws on surrogacy differ between the areas under the effective control of the Republic of Cyprus and the north of Cyprus."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Republic of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["Surrogacy is strictly limited to cases where a woman is medically unable to carry a pregnancy. In all cases, court approval is required before any treatment begins. No form of financial compensation for the surrogate is allowed. Prospective parents and clinics may face criminal charges if they proceed without the appropriate approvals in place."]},{"k":"hs","s":["The north of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pursuing surrogacy in the north carries risks as clinics are not regulated and there is no legal framework governing arrangements. Legal transfer of parenthood can be complex as the birth mother is given automatic parental responsibility. Obtaining birth certificates can also be complicated and may take a long time."]},{"k":"p","s":["The British High Commission’s ability to provide any assistance in the north of Cyprus is limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Surrogacy arrangements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before entering into any surrogacy arrangements:"]},{"k":"li","s":["read FCDO and Home Office ",["guidance on surrogacy overseas","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/surrogacy-overseas"]]},{"k":"li","s":["fully research hospitals and clinics to ensure they are safe and reputable"]},{"k":"li","s":["seek specialist legal advice both in the UK and in Cyprus"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Returning to the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["Children born through surrogacy are not automatically entitled to British nationality. To bring your child born through surrogacy from Cyprus to the UK, you must apply for a ",["full British passport","https://www.gov.uk/overseas-passports"]," for the child before you travel. The application process can take a significant amount of time, and even longer if supporting documentation has been issued in the north of Cyprus, due to the UK’s non-recognition of the authorities there. The maximum period you may be allowed to stay in Cyprus without a visa is 90 days."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Fertility treatment"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fertility treatment in the north of Cyprus carries risks as clinics are not regulated in the same way as in the UK. Make sure you conduct thorough research beforehand. Further information on fertility treatment abroad is available on the ",["Human Fertilisation and Embryology Authority (HFEA) website","https://www.hfea.gov.uk/treatments/explore-all-treatments/fertility-treatment-abroad/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["International parental child abduction"]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in the north of Cyprus do not act on British court orders. They will not share information with the British High Commission on children subject to UK court orders. This means the British High Commission cannot ensure that these children are properly safeguarded."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid taking photographs near sensitive areas, such as military buildings. If you do, the authorities could arrest you."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bathing is generally safe, but there can be strong waves and currents. Always follow warning signs and only swim on approved beaches."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Adventure sports, including quad biking"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you do any extreme or adventure sports, water sports or diving, you should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["get the right travel insurance – the sport should be included by name on your policy"]},{"k":"li","s":["always check the details of your insurance cover before you travel, including the exclusions"]},{"k":"li","s":["only use properly licensed and insured operators and satisfy yourself that adequate safety precautions are in place"]},{"k":"li","s":["never hand over your passport as a guarantee against the return of equipment"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ⁠ ",["watersports safety advice","https://www.rospa.com/leisure-water-safety/water/advice/watersports-abroad"]," from the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents (ROSPA)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Cyprus, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Cyprus guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/cyprus/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You could be heavily fined if you:"]},{"k":"li","s":["drive or are a passenger without wearing a seat belt"]},{"k":"li","s":["ride a motorbike without a crash helmet"]},{"k":"li","s":["use a mobile while driving"]},{"k":"li","s":["are found to be drink-driving"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Licences and permits"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Cyprus. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must display a UK sticker to drive a UK-based car in Cyprus."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Cyprus, you may be able to use your ",["Blue Badge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/blue-badge-using-it-in-the-eu/using-a-blue-badge-in-the-european-union"]," in some circumstances. Check local parking rules."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving standards are poor and there is an increased risk of road deaths compared to the UK."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Car and motorbike hire"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cars hired in the Republic of Cyprus often have no insurance cover in the north of the island. Check with your insurance company as you will not be allowed through a crossing without the correct insurance documents. At some crossing points it is possible to buy car insurance for the north of Cyprus. This includes the Agios Dometios."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire companies renting mopeds and quad bikes usually only provide third-party insurance, so you will have to pay for any damage to the vehicle. If you do not pay, you could be arrested."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you hire a vehicle, check:"]},{"k":"li","s":["it is in good condition and roadworthy"]},{"k":"li","s":["it has appropriate safety equipment"]},{"k":"p","s":["Rental companies often ask for your passport as a deposit, which is illegal. Never hand over your passport in this way."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Department of Meteorology of Cyprus issues warnings for severe weather, especially during summer. Weather can sometimes cause travel disruption. Monitor local and international updates and severe weather warnings from the Cyprus Department of Meteorology, and check with your travel provider if necessary. Always follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wildfires"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high risk of wildfires during the summer season from April to October. Ensure that your mobile phone is registered to receive emergency alerts to be warned of wildfires near your location."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wildfires are highly dangerous and unpredictable. The situation can change quickly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be cautious if you are in or near an area affected by wildfires:"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow the guidance of the emergency services"]},{"k":"li","s":["call the Cyprus emergency services on 112 if you are in immediate danger"]},{"k":"p","s":["Get more information on wildfires from the Department of Forests website."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cyprus experiences earthquakes and tremors. Make sure you know safety procedures and follow advice given by the local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Cyprus. It only covers regions where the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel to the north of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Republic of Cyprus is a full EU member, but the country is divided by the ‘Green Line’. The British government does not recognise the self-declared ‘Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus’."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Consular assistance in the north of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO can only provide limited consular support if you are visiting areas in the north that are not under control of the Republic of Cyprus. The authorities in the north of Cyprus do not share information with the British High Commission regarding the location, wellbeing or treatment of British nationals. This means you may be more vulnerable and at greater risk."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Recognition of documents from the north of Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Republic of Cyprus does not recognise residence permits issued in the north of Cyprus."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Cyprus","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/61/cyprus#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Cyprus","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/61/cyprus"],", including biting insects and ticks."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cyprus-list-of-hospitals"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Cyprus, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-in-cyprus"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Cyprus"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 112 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers in Cyprus","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Cyprus"]," and ",["lawyers in the north of Cyprus","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find?country=northern%20Cyprus&serviceType=lawyers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["funeral directors in Cyprus","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Cyprus"]," and ",["funeral directors in the north of Cyprus","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find?country=northern%20Cyprus&serviceType=funeralDirectors"]]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["translators and interpreters in Cyprus","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find?country=Cyprus&serviceType=translatorsInterpreters"]," and ",["translators and interpreters in the north of Cyprus","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find?country=northern%20Cyprus&serviceType=translatorsInterpreters"]]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cyprus-bereavement-information"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Cyprus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cyprus-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/cyprus/british-high-commission-nicosia"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cyprus/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":20543,"change":"New information about fertility treatment (‘Safety and security’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/moroccos-deadly-wall-across-western-sahara-why-the-2-700km-berm-was-built-and-the-damage-it-does-292148":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"e8324cb0f07180daa548f7812d8d754cc371f671fdb8cd4acf46c322afc4e071","read":"https://theconversation.com/moroccos-deadly-wall-across-western-sahara-why-the-2-700km-berm-was-built-and-the-damage-it-does-292148","bytes":188769,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Morocco’s deadly wall across Western Sahara: why the 2,700km Berm was built and the damage it does","by":[{"n":"Joana Castro Pereira","r":"Professor of Politics and International Relations, Universidade do Porto"},{"n":"Simón Pardo de Donlebún Versaci","r":"Postgraduate researcher, Universidade do Porto"}],"pub":"2026-09-23T13:49:49Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Western Sahara has been carved in two ",["since the 1980s","https://gssr.georgetown.edu/the-forum/regions/mena/blind-to-the-berm-western-sahara-morocco-and-the-worlds-quiet-abandonment-of-self-determination/"]," by a wall that Morocco built to hold on to territory it ",["occupied in 1975","https://opil.ouplaw.com/display/10.1093/law:epil/9780199231690/law-9780199231690-e233"],". Fifty years on, the conflict remains unresolved and the wall – known as the Berm – remains one of the world’s ",["most militarised","https://archives2.the-monitor.org/en-gb/reports/2022/western-sahara/view-all.aspx"]," borders. The region is the focus of a ",["project","https://africanos.eu/index.php/pt/ceaup-i-d/projetos-futuros/1288-territ%C3%B3rios-e-pol%C3%ADticas-africanas"]," at the University of Porto exploring prospects for biodiversity restoration. ",["Joana Castro Pereira","https://sigarra.up.pt/flup/en/FUNC_GERAL.FORMVIEW?p_codigo=682305"],", a professor of politics and international relations, and postgraduate researcher ",["Simón Pardo de Donlebún Versaci","https://theconversation.com/profiles/simon-pardo-de-donlebun-versaci-2765313"]," explain the history of the wall and its political and ecological risks."]},{"k":"h","s":["What is the history of the Berm?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Built between ",["1980 and 1987","https://gssr.georgetown.edu/the-forum/regions/mena/blind-to-the-berm-western-sahara-morocco-and-the-worlds-quiet-abandonment-of-self-determination/"],", the Berm separates the part of Western Sahara controlled by Morocco from the strip held by the ",["Polisario Front","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Polisario-Front"],". The Polisario Front is a movement ",["supported by Algeria","https://www.cfr.org/articles/moroccos-victory-western-sahara"]," that represents the Sahrawi people and has fought for Western Sahara’s independence since the early 1970s. It currently controls about ",["20%","https://www.cfr.org/articles/moroccos-victory-western-sahara"]," of the territory."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Western Sahara-Morocco dispute"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Berm runs for about ",["2,700 kilometres","https://archives2.the-monitor.org/en-gb/reports/2022/western-sahara/view-all.aspx"],". It’s the longest continuous barrier after the ",["Great Wall of China","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Great-Wall-of-China"],". Alongside it runs a ",["belt of landmines","https://minurso.unmissions.org/en/mine-action"]," – more than 10 million – often called the world’s longest continuous minefield."]},{"k":"p","s":["The wall was built in stages, pushing south as Morocco fought the Polisario Front for control of Western Sahara. The disputed territory is a ",["former Spanish colony","https://www.cfr.org/articles/moroccos-victory-western-sahara"]," that Madrid handed over in the 1970s without ever holding the ",["independence referendum","https://raed.academy/en/western-sahara-the-colony-spain-never-decolonized/"]," international law required."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Berm let Morocco do what battlefield victories hadn’t: keep the most valuable parts of the territory – coastline, phosphate mines and towns – while pushing Polisario fighters and hundreds of thousands of Sahrawi civilians into refugee camps in Algeria. The UN calls this the ",["world’s second-longest-running refugee crisis","https://unric.org/en/far-from-the-headlines-after-50-years-refugees-from-western-sahara-are-still-in-camps/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Morocco did not build the Berm alone. French and Israeli military strategists ",["advised","https://centredelas.org/publicacions/mundoarmurallado/?lang=en"]," on its construction."]},{"k":"h","s":["What major risks does the Berm pose?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Three kinds of harm are often overlooked amid the diplomatic dispute over the territory."]},{"k":"p","s":["The most direct is the minefield itself. The ",["Landmine and Cluster Munition Monitor","https://archives2.the-monitor.org/en-gb/reports/2022/western-sahara/view-all.aspx"],", a civil society research initiative that tracks landmines and other remnants of war, reports ",["1,427 recorded casualties","https://archives2.the-monitor.org/en-gb/reports/2022/western-sahara/view-all.aspx"]," through 2017, while noting that the total number is unknown. Many mine-related accidents go unreported, and mines can be shifted by wind or flash floods into ground already cleared."]},{"k":"p","s":["Secondly, the wall disrupts a whole desert ecosystem, not just a human community. ",["Sahrawi pastoral life","https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13570-014-0007-4"]," has always followed the wadis – the temporary rivers that flood briefly after rain and sustain wells, trees and grazing along their banks. This moves people, herds and land together across the seasons in one relational system. On one side, the wall cuts through the seasonal watercourses on which the Sahrawi depend. On the other, the embankment acts like a dam. Rainwater accumulates against it, and shrubs and grasses grow along its base. This vegetation offers food and shelter, drawing animals towards the mines laid along the wall. The very conditions that should sustain life can ",["instead direct it towards danger","https://doi.org/10.1177/02637758251347026"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The mines’ victims ",["aren’t only human","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0308275X261436183"],": camels are also killed near the wall, while deaths among wild animals may go unrecorded. These deaths are harm in themselves, not simply damage to an empty landscape."]},{"k":"p","s":["This dynamic resembles what ecologists term an ",["ecological trap","https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2004.00417.x"],", in which a habitat conveys misleading signals about its own safety. The situation at the Berm differs in a crucial respect: here the trap is produced by military infrastructure. The wall, the desert’s hydrological cycle and the minefields combine into a ",["single lethal system","https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2004.00417.x"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Thirdly, the wall cuts across the traditional grazing routes connecting wadis, with mines and unexploded ordnance ",["restricting","https://archives2.the-monitor.org/en-gb/reports/2021/western-sahara/cluster-munition-ban-policy.aspx"]," Sahrawi herders’ access to water sources that become available at different times of the year. This disrupts an ",["ancestral balance between Sahrawi communities and the desert","https://www.berghahnbooks.com/title/VolpatoDesert"],", sustained through shared seasonal rhythms, grazing practices and inherited knowledge of routes. It is an interconnected way of life that ",["forced settlement in the refugee camps is eroding","https://ethnobotanyjournal.org/index.php/era/article/view/924"],". Even dead branches are put to use rather than wasted, reflecting a careful stewardship of scarce desert resources that ",["displacement is making increasingly difficult to maintain","https://ethnobotanyjournal.org/index.php/era/article/view/924"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Almost nobody can study any of this independently since the Berm is a ",["closed military zone","https://gssr.georgetown.edu/the-forum/regions/mena/blind-to-the-berm-western-sahara-morocco-and-the-worlds-quiet-abandonment-of-self-determination/"],". Human Rights Watch has documented how tightly ",["access is controlled","https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/atf/cf/%7b65BFCF9B-6D27-4E9C-8CD3-CF6E4FF96FF9%7d/MINURSO%20HRW%20report%20December2008.pdf"],", so there’s no reliable count of animals killed, no complete contamination map, and no independent record of the damage, making accountability far harder."]},{"k":"h","s":["What does the Berm mean today in the ongoing conflict between Morocco and Western Sahara?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The wall still marks the practical frontline of a conflict ",["most of the world stopped watching","https://gssr.georgetown.edu/the-forum/regions/mena/blind-to-the-berm-western-sahara-morocco-and-the-worlds-quiet-abandonment-of-self-determination/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["A UN-brokered ceasefire held for ",["nearly 30 years","https://minurso.unmissions.org/en/background"]," until November 2020. Moroccan troops ",["entered a UN-monitored buffer strip","https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2020/11/human-rights-monitoring-needed-more-than-ever-in-western-sahara/?utm_source=chatgpt.com"]," to clear a road blockade by Sahrawi protesters and reopen traffic to Mauritania. This prompted the Polisario Front to declare the ceasefire over and ",["resume hostilities","https://www.crisisgroup.org/brf/middle-east-north-africa/western-sahara/b96-window-diplomacy-western-sahara"],". Low-intensity fighting, mostly drone strikes and skirmishes ",["along the wall","https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/news-feature/2023/05/17/morocco-sahrawi-drone-attacks?utm_source=chatgpt.com"],", has continued since."]},{"k":"p","s":["Diplomatically, the ground has ",["shifted decisively","https://theconversation.com/why-western-sahara-remains-one-of-africas-most-divisive-political-issues-114373"]," in favour of Morocco, which has been on the rise economically and politically on the continent."]},{"k":"p","s":["The US recognised Moroccan sovereignty over Western Sahara in ",["2020","https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/proclamation-recognizing-sovereignty-kingdom-morocco-western-sahara/"],". Spain backed Morocco’s autonomy plan – which would offer Western Sahara autonomy but under its sovereignty – in ",["2022","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/19/algeria-recalls-spain-envoy-over-western-sahara-policy-change"],". France backed this plan in 2024 and the UK followed in ",["2025","https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20250601-uk-morocco-western-sahara"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In October 2025, the UN security council passed ",["Resolution 2797","https://press.un.org/en/2025/sc16208.doc.htm"],", inviting both sides to ",["negotiate on the basis of Morocco’s autonomy plan","https://www.ejiltalk.org/the-security-council-and-the-western-sahara-between-self-determination-and-implicit-recognition-of-moroccan-sovereignty/"]," without calling for the referendum on independence that’s sought by the Polisario Front."]},{"k":"p","s":["That resolution also requested a review of the future of the UN’s peacekeeping mission there, ",["Minurso","https://minurso.unmissions.org/en/about-minurso"],", while extending its mandate to October 2026. The UN mission began in ",["1991","https://peacekeeping.un.org/en/factsheet/minurso"]," and was mandated to uphold the ceasefire agreed between Morocco and the Polisario Front. The review could lead to a reduced UN presence, or even its withdrawal, affecting work that includes supporting demining and recording landmine incidents."]},{"k":"p","s":["Whatever happens to the wall politically, questions remain about who is responsible for managing its lasting consequences for the people, animals and land around it."]}],"chars":6459},"https://theconversation.com/the-2-tax-hike-on-rental-income-is-bad-for-landlords-and-tenants-alike-heres-what-reform-looks-like-295816":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"d9f29ce30800bfef38c53af4230ac6bcbab39079af53a1d07921f4abf9858c38","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-2-tax-hike-on-rental-income-is-bad-for-landlords-and-tenants-alike-heres-what-reform-looks-like-295816","bytes":181554,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The 2% tax hike on rental income is bad for landlords and tenants alike. Here’s what reform looks like","by":[{"n":"Emma Laurie","r":"Emerita Fellow, University of Southampton"}],"pub":"2026-11-13T10:18:41Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The October budget brought unwelcome news for private landlords in England, Wales and Northern Ireland. From April 2027, they will pay a further 2% tax on rental income, in addition to the 2% increase announced in the 2025 budget."]},{"k":"p","s":["Given the ",["notoriously high cost of private renting","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/chapters-for-english-housing-survey-2024-to-2025-headline-findings-on-demographics-and-household-resilience/chapter-2-housing-costs-and-affordability"]," in the UK, there may be little sympathy for ",["landlords","https://theconversation.com/topics/landlords-77383"],". And the new tax rise will most likely be passed on to tenants through rent increases anyway – a view shared by both the ",["National Residential Landlords Association","https://www.nrla.org.uk/news/budget-announcements"]," and the UK’s ",["public finance watchdog","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae4a7a9ec36e258a1998484/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026-web-accessible.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["I’m a law professor specialising in social and private rental markets. I believe that the new tax represents a major missed opportunity. Instead of increasing landlords’ tax burden – and indirectly raising rents for tenants – the government could have taken the chance to negotiate with landlords to cap rent increases."]},{"k":"p","s":["But given that the new tax will probably be passed on to renters, how much power do tenants themselves have to resist rent hikes?"]},{"k":"h","s":["How to fight a rent raise"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Renters’ Rights Act 2025 was rightly hailed as a landmark change in tenants’ rights. It abolished the notorious section 21 ",["“no fault” eviction","https://england.shelter.org.uk/housing_advice/eviction/section_21_eviction"],", effective as of May 1, 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the act is very unlikely to protect tenants from a rent increase that is in line with the going rate. Landlords can only give notice to ",["raise the rent","https://england.shelter.org.uk/housing_advice/private_renting/rent_increases"]," once a year, and must provide a minimum of two months’ notice. Tenants can ",["challenge an increase","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/apply-for-an-open-market-rent-determination"]," by applying to a tribunal, but they will only be successful if the new rent is more expensive than the local market rate."]},{"k":"p","s":["Both tenants and landlords are encouraged to provide information about rents for other similar properties. There is no single approved source for such information, but websites such as Zoopla and Rightmove are typically used."]},{"k":"p","s":["Before applying to the tribunal, it is sensible to try to reach agreement over the rent. The 2025 act does give tenants much greater flexibility. They can now ",["end a tenancy","https://england.shelter.org.uk/housing_advice/private_renting/ending_a_periodic_tenancy/example_of_a_notice_to_quit"]," with two months’ notice, whereas before the act, tenancies would often have a defined duration, for example 12 months. This means that tenants are no longer tied to paying for a tenancy that has become unaffordable. It may also make landlords more willing to negotiate to keep good tenants."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the broader point remains: the 2025 act cannot protect against rent increases in areas where ",["demand outstrips supply","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c4gqjv476qeo"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["When the Renters’ Rights Act was going through Parliament, the government was adamant that rent caps would not be introduced. At the time, ",["I argued","https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1468-2230.70030"]," that it was part of an unspoken deal to win landlords’ support for abolishing section 21 evictions."]},{"k":"h","s":["How the government can protect renters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Rent controls are a ",["source of controversy","https://theconversation.com/could-new-tenants-rights-usher-in-rent-controls-heres-why-that-wouldnt-necessarily-be-a-positive-268827"]," in the UK, largely because of the legacy of measures in force for most of the 20th century. But since rents were brought out of control by Margaret Thatcher’s government in 1988, the private rental sector has adopted a classically neoliberal approach."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no universally agreed definition of neoliberalism. In housing, it generally means that private landlords should be free to charge market rents for homes that offer relatively little stability."]},{"k":"p","s":["Prime Minister Andy Burnham has ",["recently recognised","https://labour.org.uk/updates/members-updates/andy-burnham-elected-leader-of-the-labour-party/"]," that the neoliberal model “simply doesn’t work well enough for ordinary people”. What, then, is the alternative?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Instead of imposing the additional 2% tax on landlords, the government could have taken a first step to making renting more affordable by getting landlord support to cap rent increases. In other words, a trade-off between tax income, benefiting the government, and rent capping, which would potentially benefit tenants."]},{"k":"p","s":["Rent control measures have become increasingly sophisticated, but they are certainly ",["not a silver bullet","https://theconversation.com/rent-control-on-its-own-wont-solve-the-housing-crisis-207353"]," for the housing crisis."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, reducing landlords’ tax obligations in exchange for modest price capping – for example by tying rises to the Consumer Prices Index, ",["as Scotland has done","https://www.gov.scot/policies/private-renting/rent-controls/"]," – could work for landlords and renters alike."]},{"k":"p","s":["Landlords have long expressed dissatisfaction with the numerous changes to their tax position since 2015. They do have a point: cumulative changes have made the English tax and regulation regime one of the ",["least favourable","https://www.nrla.org.uk/research/deep-insight/lse-2021-intl-tax"]," in Europe and North America."]},{"k":"p","s":["Any such deal would, of course, mean the government giving up the ",["expected additional tax income","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/income-tax-changes-to-tax-rates-for-property-and-savings-income/income-tax-changes-to-tax-rates-for-property-and-savings-income"]," – estimated at £35 million in 2027-28 and £445 million by 2030-31. But if the prime minister is serious about moving away from a rental housing market dominated by ",["four decades of neoliberal thinking","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cz7djw5dznpo"],", this proposal would represent a modest first step in the right direction – one that would benefit everyone."]}],"chars":4620},"https://theconversation.com/the-uncomfortable-question-at-the-heart-of-europes-identity-crisis-293136":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"22f008df053526b15c9b0b7aaf5e141c857566cbffef4506a4ff664e46009f9b","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-uncomfortable-question-at-the-heart-of-europes-identity-crisis-293136","bytes":177792,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The uncomfortable question at the heart of Europe’s identity crisis","by":[{"n":"Kristoffer Bayer","r":"PhD candidate, Department of Philosophy and History of Ideas, Aarhus University"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T15:19:34Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["What makes someone French, German, British or Danish? Is it the passport they hold, the language they speak, or the ancestry they inherit?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Across Europe, parts of the nationalist right are increasingly blurring the distinction between culture and ethnicity. The result is a view of national identity where belonging depends on where a person’s ancestors come from."]},{"k":"p","s":["This way of thinking is called ",["ethnonationalism","https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1468-4446.12325"]," – where membership of a nation depends on ethnic background or ancestry, rather than simply on citizenship or cultural identity."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Denmark, where I live, this idea has recently taken a striking turn. A few months ago, I appeared in a televised debate opposite a former political adviser to a Danish right-wing party who, in essence and quite seriously, argued that to be Danish, ",["you have to be able to tolerate milk","https://www.berlingske.dk/laesere/danskhed-sidder-ikke-i-blodet-og-slet-ikke-i-et-glas-maelk?gaa_at=eafs&gaa_n=AXbw80TnZiNz2bDsfcI-z1AX-lcZujMMojAls05NKMf51bn_oovz0sHjFBQPGISAwcc%3D&gaa_ts=6ad8accf&gaa_sig=ldn-S3AIvP4ULg5Lrwe5BHV8yvfdwNpMN985QO-eyCw6PYLlWKPzDLQDCSKhOwLXGi9YIHvxPtEsWRKcM9vkrA%3D%3D"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The argument draws on the fact that the ability to digest lactose, the sugar in milk, is ",["more common","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/science-nature/famine-and-diseases-likely-drove-europeans-ability-to-digest-milk-180980483/"]," among people with Northern European ancestry. The suggestion was that this biological trait could help define who is truly Danish."]},{"k":"p","s":["Others on the nationalist right soon made similar arguments. One commentator ",["wrote","https://www.berlingske.dk/kommentatorer/danskhed-er-ikke-blod--det-er-aand-skrev-christian-egander-skov-men-logikken-er-hullet-som-en-si"]," that Danish ancestry should be the basis for defining Danishness, because cultural “spirit” ultimately derives from blood."]},{"k":"p","s":["But while the ability to digest milk might seem an absurd test of Danishness, the underlying question is a serious one and it extends far beyond Denmark."]},{"k":"h","s":["When citizenship isn’t enough"]},{"k":"p","s":["Similar ideas are gaining ground elsewhere in Europe. In France, ",["Marine Le Pen’s nationalist political project","https://academic.oup.com/book/41499/chapter-abstract/352912204?redirectedFrom=fulltext&"]," has promoted an ethnonationalist view of national identity, in which ancestry plays a central role in defining who belongs."]},{"k":"p","s":["While Germany’s far-right Alternative for Germany (AfD) has also made ",["ethnonationalist claims","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-race-ethnicity-and-politics/article/conspiring-a-conspiracy-theory-from-umvolkung-to-remigration-and-the-alternative-for-germanys-political-mobilization-of-population-replacement-conspiracy-theories/19021FDE51098F62F6E60126D9B9A5BB"]," as it has gained ground. In 2025, Germany’s domestic intelligence agency classified the party as ",["right-wing extremist","https://www.information.dk/udland/2025/05/alternative-fur-deutschland-faaet-ekstremiststemplet-faa-debatten-partiet-eksplodere"],", although the legal status and implications of that classification have been contested."]},{"k":"p","s":["These movements differ, but they raise a common question: can someone be a citizen of a country, live there for years and participate in its society, yet still be considered an outsider because of their ancestry?"]},{"k":"p","s":["This distinction between legal citizenship and supposedly authentic national belonging is central to the ",["nationalist right’s calls","https://voxeurop.eu/en/remigration-slogan-mass-deportations-far-right"]," for “remigration” across Europe."]},{"k":"p","s":[["The term is used by proponents","https://www.diis.dk/publikationer/betyder-remigration-store-udskiftning"]," to argue that people with migrant backgrounds should be removed from countries where they live, even when they have citizenship or were born there. This can include ",["calling for the mass deportation","https://www.tagesschau.de/inland/gesellschaft/afd-remigration-100.html"]," of people deemed not to belong to the nation because of their ethnicity, ancestry or cultural background."]},{"k":"p","s":["Such ideas often overlap with the so-called “great replacement” ",["conspiracy theory","https://www.youtube.com/shorts/7-Ulsus8yY4"],", which claims those with non-Western backgrounds are replacing existing populations. And elements of this narrative have ",["been adopted by nationalist and far-right politicians","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/13548565221091983"]," across Europe."]},{"k":"h","s":["Something has changed"]},{"k":"p","s":["The word “ethnocultural” brings together two ideas: that a nation has a distinctive culture and that this culture belongs to a particular ethnic group. It suggests that national identity is not simply something people can learn or participate in, but something rooted in their origins."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some researchers ",["describe the shift","https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0031322X.2021.1920722"]," in how nationalist politicians talk about national identity as a “ ",["cultural turn","https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13569317.2023.2212252"],"”. Rather than focusing explicitly on race or ancestry, they increasingly talk about protecting a country’s culture, values and way of life."]},{"k":"p","s":["On the surface, that might sound more inclusive; people can learn the language or adopt new customs. But if a country’s culture is treated as something you inherit from your ancestors, rather than something you can share and take part in, the boundary between insiders and outsiders remains."]},{"k":"h","s":["European values?"]},{"k":"p","s":[["My research in Denmark","https://pure.au.dk/portal/da/publications/danish-nationalism-at-the-intersection-of-freedom-democracy-and-e/"]," has examined how national identity has changed over time. The findings suggest that, despite what some nationalist political groups argue, most people do not see ancestry as the defining feature of national belonging."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Danish Values Study","https://www.rigsarkivet.dk/vejledning/den-danske-vaerdiundersoegelse-1981-2017/"],", part of a wider European survey, asks people about their beliefs and values. Comparing responses from the most recent 2008 and 2017 surveys, the proportion who thought being Danish was mainly about having Danish roots fell from 19% to 15%."]},{"k":"p","s":["Meanwhile, around four in five saw being Danish as a matter of belonging to the country and its society rather than having a particular ethnic background. This figure remained almost unchanged, at 77% in 2008 and 79% in 2017. These findings offer a different perspective from the idea that national identity is rooted in blood (or milk)."]},{"k":"p","s":["This understanding also has roots in history. The Danish theologian NFS Grundtvig expressed a vision of national belonging in his 1848 poem ",["Folkeligheden","https://danmarkshistorien.lex.dk/N.F.S._Grundtvig_%E2%80%9CFolkeligheden%E2%80%9D,_1848"],": “To a people all belong, who count themselves as part of it”. In other words, belonging is something people can claim for themselves, rather than something dictated by family background."]},{"k":"p","s":["That said, the poem has featured in debates about Danish identity, with figures on the right using it to argue for a narrower definition of national belonging, while others see it as an argument for a more inclusive idea of Danishness."]},{"k":"p","s":["And ultimately this raises the broader question: who has the legitimacy to define who belongs to a country or not? A question that will likely be a defining feature of European politics for years to come."]},{"k":"p","s":["This article was commissioned as part of a partnership between ",["Videnskab.dk","https://videnskab.dk/"]," and The Conversation. Articles are also published in Danish."]}],"chars":5584},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:47Z","sha":"c077710f55139a446aabf396238e86889f5ff7ca1ac4e9ead4b38e9409c8a296","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/japan","bytes":95431,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Japan travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-01T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-28T16:22:35+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Global travel impacts due to escalation in the Middle East"]},{"k":"p","s":["Escalation in the Middle East has caused widespread travel disruption, including airspace closures, delayed and cancelled flights."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel plans may be affected, even if your destination is not in the Middle East. Before you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check travel advice for any countries or territories you are transiting through"]},{"k":"li","s":["check for the latest updates from your airline or tour operator"]},{"k":"li","s":["review your travel insurance policy for coverage"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local and international media for the latest information and sign up for travel advice email alerts"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Japan set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Japanese Embassy in the UK","https://www.uk.emb-japan.go.jp/itprtop_en/index.html"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re visiting Japan, your passport must be valid for the length of your stay. No additional period of validity is required. You need a blank page for your visa stamp."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get a visa on arrival in Japan for tourism or business for up to 90 days. You do not need to apply before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need a multiple-entry visa, you must ask the immigration officials when you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to stay longer, you can apply at your nearest immigration office for an extension for another 90 days. Your passport must be valid for the period of the extension."]},{"k":"p","s":["For long-term stays or to work or study, you must meet the Japanese government’s entry requirements. Check ",["which type of visa you need","https://www.mofa.go.jp/j_info/visit/visa/long/index.html"],". It is illegal to work in Japan without the correct visa no matter how informal or temporary the work."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you overstay your permission to remain in Japan, you risk arrest, detention and a heavy fine."]},{"k":"p","s":["For residency information, see the Japanese Immigration Services Agency website and read about ",["living in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-japan"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Japan guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/114/japan#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Japan","https://www.customs.go.jp/english/summary/prohibit.htm"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Japan"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to bring meat products including sausages, bacon and ham to Japan without permission from the ",["Japanese Animal Quarantine Service","https://www.maff.go.jp/aqs/english/contactus.html"],". Penalties include a heavy fine and prison sentence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Whale meat is available in Japan but importing it into the UK and EU is illegal. If you import whale meat to the UK, you can get a fine of up to £5,000 and a prison sentence. Customs officers will seize the meat."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Japan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Japan is still a predominantly cash-based society. You may have difficulty using credit and debit cards issued outside Japan. Cirrus, Maestro, Link and Delta cash cards are not widely accepted. Japanese post offices, 7-Eleven stores and JP Post Bank have cash machines that will accept some foreign cards during business hours."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Japan","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Japan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Japan, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Civil disturbance and violent demonstrations are rare in Japan. Occasionally, there are pro-nationalist demonstrations that express hostility towards foreign countries. If you become aware of protests, leave the area immediately."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Japan and the Korean Peninsula"]},{"k":"p","s":["The level of tension and security situation in the Korean Peninsula can change with little notice. Tensions can rise after missile tests by North Korea and during the regular South Korea-US military exercises, which take place throughout the year."]},{"k":"p","s":["North Korea frequently launches missiles towards Japan as part of missile testing, and is likely to continue doing so."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should follow the advice of local authorities and check ",["NHK World","https://www3.nhk.or.jp/nhkworld/"]," for the latest information. See ",["advice on missile alerts from the Japanese government","https://www.kokuminhogo.go.jp/en/pc-index_e.html"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["See also ",["travel advice for South Korea","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/south-korea"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take the same precautions you would at home and get local advice on areas where you might need to be more alert."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tokyo’s entertainment districts carry a higher risk of crime, particularly at night in and around clubs and bars. Foreign nationals have been targeted for extortion, robbery, assault and sexual assault."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware of your surroundings and keep your belongings in sight when travelling. Place valuables in a secure place such as a hotel safe when you can."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rape and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Rape and sexual assault are rare but can happen. Japanese law puts a high burden of proof on victims to demonstrate sexual relations were not consensual and committed through assault, intimidation or force."]},{"k":"p","s":["Female passengers travelling on commuter trains have experienced inappropriate behaviour. This includes touching and upskirting – taking photos or videos from below when women walk or stand nearby. Police advise you should shout at the perpetrator to attract attention and ask a fellow passenger to call train or station staff."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking and credit card fraud"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are increased reports of drink spiking and credit card fraud. Victims have described waking up with no memory of what happened and discovering large amounts of money billed to their credit card. The amount of money taken ranges from hundreds to tens of thousands of pounds. Risks are higher in all entertainment and nightlife districts across Japan. In particular, the Japanese police have identified the following areas in Tokyo as high-risk zones:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Kabukicho"]},{"k":"li","s":["Roppongi"]},{"k":"li","s":["Shibuya"]},{"k":"li","s":["Ikebukuro"]},{"k":"p","s":["Most British victims have reported being targeted in Kabukicho, a popular tourist destination in Shinjuku. Victims claim to be approached in English by street touts known as “kyakuhiki”. Do not follow street touts into bars."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can reduce the risk and impact of spiking by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["not accepting drinks from strangers"]},{"k":"li","s":["not leaving drinks unattended or asking a trusted friend to keep an eye on your unfinished drink"]},{"k":"li","s":["not following street touts into bars"]},{"k":"li","s":["securing your finances against fraudulent transactions"]},{"k":"li","s":["only carrying what you need and securing spare cash, cards, and valuables at your accommodation"]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information on drink spiking, and how to reduce the risk, please visit our travel aware website ",["here","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Japan has strict laws around drug use. If the police find an illegal substance in your bloodstream, you may be charged, regardless of whether you consented or were aware of taking the drugs. If you refuse to take a test if asked to by the police, you could be arrested and detained for ‘obstructing police duties’."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you take blood tests at medical institutions, they are usually obliged to report to the police if they detect any illegal substances."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Disputes over bar bills"]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals have been arrested at clubs and bars following disputes with staff and doorpersons over excessive bar bills. These disputes are often seen in the high risk areas outlined above. Ensure you see a menu with prices before you enter a bar or restaurant. Keep track of what you order and confirm the price of admission to clubs on entry."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Prostitution and street touts"]},{"k":"p","s":["Prostitution and street touts are illegal but commonplace, especially in the high risk areas outlined above. Do not accompany street touts to bars or clubs. Street touts often encourage people to enter an establishment by misrepresenting the services on offer. You may incur an entry charge so do not enter on the promise of a free drink or to have a look, as you may not be free to leave."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Legal system in Japan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Japanese and UK law are very different. In Japan, you can be arrested and detained without charge for up to 23 days, even for minor offences such as theft or trespassing."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must always carry your passport or Japanese residence card with you. The police can arrest you if you cannot show proof of your visa or residence status."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol bans"]},{"k":"p","s":["In general, drinking alcohol in public places is not illegal. However, there are bans on drinking alcohol in specific areas on certain days, for example New Year’s Eve."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Smoking in public places"]},{"k":"p","s":["Smoking is illegal on the streets of Tokyo and some other cities. Smoke only in designated areas."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is zero tolerance of drug crime and the penalties are severe. British nationals have been arrested and detained for receiving small quantities through the post or if they test positive when the police raid clubs or bars. British nationals convicted of drug trafficking have received sentences ranging from 6 to 17 years and fines of 3 to 4 million yen. Prisoners in Japan are assigned labour or factory work as part of their sentences."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is not illegal, but Japanese law does not protect against discrimination on the grounds of sexual orientation. Same-sex marriages are not recognised, but some local authorities issue certificates of recognition that can be used for civil issues, such as hospital visitation rights."]},{"k":"p","s":["Shinjuku Nichome in Tokyo and Doyamacho in Osaka are well-known LGBT+ areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Medication bans"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to possess or use some common prescription and over-the-counter medicines under Japan’s strictly enforced law on anti-stimulant drugs. Ignorance of the law does not count as a defence. See ",["Health","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan/health"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Custody disputes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Japanese family law is different from UK law. Parents may choose joint custody of a child after divorce, or they may choose for one parent to hold sole custody. Access to a child of the non-custodial parent can be difficult. Legal custody disputes can be lengthy and expensive, and rulings to return or secure access to a child may take time to be enforced. See ",["child abduction, custody and parental rights in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/japan-child-abduction"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public behaviour"]},{"k":"p","s":["Most Japanese people are welcoming and friendly, but can be reserved. Loud, boisterous behaviour may receive negative attention. Showing affection in public is less common than in the UK."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tattoos"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tattoos have a historical association with organised crime in Japan. While acceptance is increasing, some public facilities do not admit people with tattoos – for example, public swimming pools, hot springs, beaches and some gyms. Other establishments request you cover tattoos while using the facilities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wildlife"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bear sightings and attacks have increased in parts of Japan, especially in mountainous and forested areas, including near populated zones."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow local advice and alerts, and take precautions if travelling in these areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should"]},{"k":"li","s":["research the region to learn about the local wildlife"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking alone in areas where bears have been sighted"]},{"k":"li","s":["take all rubbish with you, including food waste"]},{"k":"li","s":["report any sightings to local authorities"]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re planning to drive in Japan, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the rules of the road in the ",["Japan Automobile Federation driving guide","https://english.jaf.or.jp/driving-in-japan"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll need the ",["1949 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," plus your UK driving licence and insurance documents with you in the car. You could be arrested and fined for driving without the correct documents."]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel. You can use an IDP for one year, regardless of its expiry date. See ",["information on driving and the IDP from the Tokyo police","https://www.keishicho.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/multilingual/english/for_visitors/index.html"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to stay longer than one year, see how to ",["apply for a Japanese driving licence","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-japan#driving-in-japan"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience and a minimum age."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are 2 types of driving insurance available in Japan: compulsory insurance (‘jibaisekihoken’) and voluntary insurance (‘nin’i no jidoshahoken’). The compulsory insurance on its own may be insufficient in cases of personal liability."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Japan, and the legal limit of alcohol in your system is about a third of the UK limit. If you are found to be over the limit, you may get a fine and possible imprisonment. There are also penalties for allowing someone to drink and drive. The passenger could face arrest if the police suspect they were aware of the driver’s alcohol consumption."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["Roads are well maintained, and driving is on the left. Watch out for:"]},{"k":"li","s":["pedestrians crossing roads at green lights, especially at junctions"]},{"k":"li","s":["cyclists riding on the wrong side of the road and without lights at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["other drivers braking suddenly"]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to natural disasters in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-japan#natural-disasters"],". The Japan National Tourism Organisation operates a 24 hours a day, 7 days a week Japan Visitor Hotline for emergency English-language assistance during natural disasters: +81(5)0 3816 2787."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Extreme Heat"]},{"k":"p","s":["Japanese summers are extremely hot and humid, with temperatures often exceeding 35 degrees in July and August. High humidity often makes it feel much hotter. As a result, heat stroke and heat-related illnesses pose a significant risk during the summer months."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Japanese Environmental Agency","https://www.wbgt.env.go.jp/en/"]," publishes a heat stroke index, that identifies areas have a heat stroke alert currently in effect. The ",["website also provides information","https://www.wbgt.env.go.jp/en/doc_prevention.php"]," about heat stroke, its symptoms, and practical advice on how to prevent heat-related illnesses."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tropical cyclones"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Japan the tropical cyclone (typhoon) season is between June and December, peaking between July and September. The highest risk is in southern parts of the country. Monitor any approaching storms using Japan Meteorological Agency forecasts. Follow the advice of the local authorities and emergency services, including any evacuation orders."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tropical cyclones in Japan often come with damaging high tides, increasing the risk of landslides and flooding. The dangers increase when an earthquake occurs shortly after a tropical cyclone has saturated an area."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["weather safety tips","https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/weather/w01.html"]," from the Japan National Tourism Agency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding and heavy rain"]},{"k":"p","s":["Periods of heavy rainfall are common in Japan, particularly during the rainy season and typhoon season. Intense rain can lead to flooding, landslides, and significant disruption to transport and infrastructure. Conditions can worsen rapidly and with little warning, especially in low-lying, coastal, and mountainous areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Japan Meteorological Agency","https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/indexe.html"]," publishes weather warnings and emergency alerts that identify areas currently at risk from heavy rain, flooding, and landslides. The website also provides information on severe weather hazards, evacuation guidance, and practical advice on how to stay safe during periods of heavy rainfall."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes and tsunamis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Japan is in a major earthquake zone. The ",["Japan Meteorological Agency","https://www.bousai.go.jp/kokusai/web/img/02_bousai_guidebook_Web_EN.pdf"]," provides a list of ",["helpful apps and websites in the event of disaster","https://www.bousai.go.jp/kokusai/web/img/02_bousai_guidebook_Web_EN.pdf"],". See further ",["earthquake safety tips","https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/earthquake/e01.html"]," from the Japan National Tourism Agency."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor ",["earthquake information, including tsunami and aftershock warnings","https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/indexe.html"],", from the Japan Meteorological Agency. See further news on earthquakes at ",["NHK World","https://www3.nhk.or.jp/nhkworld/en/news/tags/23/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take note of instructions in hotel rooms and at train stations and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanic eruptions"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are several active volcanoes in Japan. Monitor local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities. Check for volcano warnings from the Japanese Meteorological Agency. See ",["volcano safety tips","https://www.jnto.go.jp/safety-tips/eng/emergency/volcanic/v01.html"]," from the Japan National Tourism Agency."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Japan. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Fukushima"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a ",["restricted area around the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant","https://www.meti.go.jp/english/earthquake/nuclear/roadmap/"]," where only authorised persons can enter. Areas where evacuation orders are ready to be lifted are still subject to some restrictions – for example, visitors are not allowed to stay overnight. Follow local guidance."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Japanese authorities carry out comprehensive checks to monitor radiation in the area around Fukushima Daiichi and to monitor possible contamination of water and ",["food and produce","https://www.maff.go.jp/e/policies/food_safety/index.html"],". They impose strict controls where necessary."]},{"k":"p","s":["Although the decommissioning of the Fukushima Daiichi site and full clean-up of the surrounding area will take many years, the risks are gradually declining."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have appropriate travel insurance for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 119 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Japan","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/114/japan#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Japan","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/114/japan"]," including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["biting insects and ticks"]},{"k":"li","s":["pertussis (‘whooping cough’)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Japan. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/disease/12/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to carry some common prescription and over-the-counter medicines under Japan’s strictly enforced law on anti-stimulant drugs. This includes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Vicks inhalers"]},{"k":"li","s":["medicines for allergies and sinus problems"]},{"k":"li","s":["cold and flu medication containing pseudoephedrine"]},{"k":"li","s":["some over-the-counter painkillers containing codeine"]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals have been detained and deported for these offences. If you’re travelling with medication, check its status with the ",["Japanese Embassy in the UK","https://www.uk.emb-japan.go.jp/itprtop_en/index.html"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Japan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities in Japan are of a high quality, but treatment is expensive. Expect to pay the whole cost of any treatment you receive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities may check your insurance, which could delay your treatment."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/japan-list-of-doctors-and-medical-facilities"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-japan#healthcare-in-japan"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Japan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 119"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 119"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 110"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Japan"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Japan"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Japan"]," in Japan"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/what-to-do-after-a-british-person-dies-in-japan"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/japan-prisoner-pack"]," in Japan"]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/japan/british-embassy-tokyo"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/japan/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Japan","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-japan"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":21950,"change":"Addition of information about flooding and heavy rain ('Safety and security' page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/norway":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"4f91c2f2a5852bcb67ff69c1adf7090f012902b98e113fe767a8939b4d98a4d0","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/norway","bytes":65635,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Norway travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-20T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-25T09:47:40+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/norway/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Norway set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how Norway’s entry requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Norwegian Embassy in the UK","https://www.norway.no/en/uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Norway follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid British passport or ",["proof of right of abode","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode"],". See ",["Dual Citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can travel without a visa to the Schengen area, which includes Norway, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The requirements for ",["working in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-norway-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days. If you overstay the 90-day visa-free limit, you may be banned from entering Schengen countries for up to 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Norwegian border control, you may also need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your accommodation, for example, a hotel booking confirmation or proof of address for a second home"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your travel insurance"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay – the amount varies depending on your accommodation"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, you must meet the Norwegian government’s entry requirements. Check which type of visa or work permit you need with the ",["Norwegian Embassy in the UK","https://www.norway.no/en/uk/services-info/visitors-visa-res-permit/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in Norway with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["entry processes if you live in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-norway#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Svalbard"]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll need your passport to travel to and enter the Svalbard archipelago."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Norway guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/168/norway#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Norway","https://www.toll.no/en/travelling-to-and-from-norway/travel-to-norway/"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to import whale meat from Norway into the UK. You could get a fine of up to £5,000 or a prison sentence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Russian vehicle restrictions"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are ",["restrictions on vehicles with Russian licence plates","https://www.regjeringen.no/en/aktuelt/norway-is-tightening-its-restrictive-measures-against-russia/id2996416/"]," in Norway."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Norway"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EEA countries","https://www.toll.no/en/goods/food/regulations-for-meat-milk-cheese-and-other-foods/"]," without special permission. There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Norway"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the ",["value is 25,000 Norwegian krone or more","https://www.toll.no/en/goods/currency/"],". If you do not declare it, you could get a substantial fine and temporary confiscation of the excess money, which may then be released only through a bank."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Norway","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/norway/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Norway"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Norway cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations in Norway are usually peaceful. If you’re near areas where demonstrations are taking place, follow the advice of local authorities and move away if there are signs of disorder."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["There’s a small risk of petty theft, particularly at airports and railway stations in and around Oslo."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, carry severe penalties. You should expect a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Norway have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drones"]},{"k":"p","s":["To fly any type of drone in Norway you need to be registered as an operator in either Norway or an EU/EFTA country and have liability insurance. Other conditions apply and certain areas are no fly zones. Familiarise yourself with local requirements at ",["Norwegian civil aviation authority website","https://www.luftfartstilsynet.no/en/drones/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"p","s":["Get information about the risk of avalanches from the Norwegian Avalanche Warning Service or the European Avalanche Warning Services."]},{"k":"p","s":["Police enforce bans on campfires in many hiking areas during the summer months."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Norway, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Norway guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/norway/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Norway for up to 90 days. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Norway, you may be able to use your ",["Blue Badge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/blue-badge-using-it-in-the-eu/using-a-blue-badge-in-the-european-union"]," in some circumstances. Check local parking rules."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Norway. If you are tested and found to have a quarter of England’s legal limit of alcohol in your system you may get a fine and possible imprisonment. There are frequent roadside checks for alcohol."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must use winter tyres with a minimum 3-millimetre (mm) tread if there is snow or ice covering the roads. You may also need to use studded tyres or snow chains. Truck drivers must use winter tyres with a minimum 5mm tread and have enough snow chains."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get the ",["full tyre requirements from Norway’s State Highways","https://www.vegvesen.no/en/vehicles/own-and-maintain/tyre-requirements/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Keep your headlights on, but dipped, during the daytime all year round in all places."]},{"k":"p","s":["On roads that are not marked with a priority sign (yellow diamond), you must give way to traffic coming from the right."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you commit a driving offence, you could get an on-the-spot fine of up to 10,000 Norwegian krone."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Arctic travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Nearly half of Norway is in the Arctic Circle, including some very remote areas of land and sea. Emergency medical assistance and search and rescue are limited in these areas. See ",["Arctic travel safety advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/arctic-travel-safety-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Extreme weather, floods and landslides can happen. You should follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Climate and temperature"]},{"k":"p","s":["The weather can change quickly, producing Arctic conditions even in summer on exposed mountain tops. Extreme weather can cause floods and landslides."]},{"k":"p","s":["The winter is long and can last well into April. Temperatures can drop to -25°C and below. There is also a high wind chill factor, particularly in unsheltered areas and mountain ranges. Weather conditions can worsen quickly."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wildfires"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of wildfires, especially during hot and dry periods. Police enforce bans on campfires in many areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Norway. It only covers regions where the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/norway"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/norway/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Svalbard"]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow the safety advice of the Governor of Svalbard, including on how to protect yourself from polar bear attacks, the risks of glaciers, avalanches and other dangers outside the main town of Longyearbyen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 113 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest information ",["vaccine recommendations for Norway","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/168/norway#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Norway","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/168/norway"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Norway"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a UK national, you can use your UK passport to get free state-provided medical treatment in hospitals. Some doctors may charge for their services elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["Access to free healthcare is not an alternative to travel insurance. Read more about ",["what your travel insurance should cover","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/norway-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Norway, you can ",["now also use the Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)","https://www.nhsbsa.nhs.uk/get-healthcare-cover-travelling-abroad/where-you-can-use-your-card"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-norway#healthcare"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Norway"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 110"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 112"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 113"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Norway"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Norway"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Norway"]," in Norway"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/norway-bereavement-packinformation"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/norway-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/norway/british-embassy-oslo"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/norway/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Norway","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-norway"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":15952,"change":"Removal of information about an ongoing air traffic controllers' strike ('Warnings and insurance' page)."}};
